{"id":"task-scheduler","title":"Task scheduler","difficulty":"medium","pattern":"greedy by counting","statement":"<p>Tasks are letters; each takes one time unit. The same letter must be separated by at least n units (idle units allowed). Return the minimum total time to run all tasks.</p>","entry":{"python":"least_interval","cpp":{"name":"leastInterval","ret":"int","params":[["string","tasks"],["int","n"]]}},"kind":"function","compare":"exact","time_limit_ms":2000,"hints":["The most frequent task dictates the skeleton: m copies need (m \u2212 1) gaps of length n.","If there are more tasks than slots, there's no idle time at all."],"complexity":"O(len(tasks))","starter":{"python":"def least_interval(tasks, n):\n    pass\n","cpp":"int leastInterval(string tasks, int n) {\n    \n}\n"},"tests":[{"args":["AAABBB",2],"expected":8,"sample":true},{"args":["AAABBB",0],"expected":6,"sample":true},{"args":["AAAAAABCDEFG",2],"expected":16},{"args":["A",5],"expected":1},{"args":["",7],"expected":10000},{"args":["",20],"expected":43156},{"args":["",3],"expected":16789}],"solution":{"explanation":"<p>A simulation with a max-heap works (always run the most frequent available task), but the counting argument is cleaner: the most frequent task (m occurrences) forces (m \u2212 1) full frames of n + 1 slots, plus one slot per task tied for the maximum in the last frame. If the tasks overflow the frames, they fill every gap and the answer is just len(tasks). Answer = max(len, (m \u2212 1)(n + 1) + k). Prove both bounds are achievable: that's the interview conversation.</p>","python":"from collections import Counter\n\ndef least_interval(tasks, n):\n    cnt = Counter(tasks).values()\n    m = max(cnt)\n    k = sum(1 for c in cnt if c == m)\n    return max(len(tasks), (m - 1) * (n + 1) + k)\n","cpp":"int leastInterval(string t, int n) {\n    int c[26] = {}; for (char ch : t) c[ch - 'A']++;\n    int m = *max_element(c, c + 26), k = count(c, c + 26, m);\n    return max((int)t.size(), (m - 1) * (n + 1) + k);\n}\n"}}